00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:26.880
about scale and capacity.

00:13:26.880 --> 00:13:30.090
When you start thinking about
centralizing your information

00:13:30.090 --> 00:13:33.330
and now making your
organization think differently,

00:13:33.330 --> 00:13:35.310
what's important
for you to realize

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:38.160
is that this is not
a one-time project.

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:40.110
It's a journey.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.930
And pretty soon you'll will
be listening from Sanjiv.

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:46.350
The journey between myself
and Sanjiv and Vikram

00:13:46.350 --> 00:13:49.560
started, really, with
the conversation around,

00:13:49.560 --> 00:13:53.410
why do you need to worry
about all this infrastructure?

00:13:53.410 --> 00:13:55.440
Why not centralize
the data, at least

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:57.580
for your specific
business problems?

00:13:57.580 --> 00:13:59.000
And let's start it there.

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:02.610
With something as simple
as that, to see them evolve

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:03.510
is amazing.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:06.960
Cisco is a fantastic company
with all kinds of complexity.

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:10.470
And when you start getting
into this kind of journey,

00:14:10.470 --> 00:14:14.640
it's very important that one,
you select the right platform.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:18.210
Two, you blend the domain
knowledge and expertise

00:14:18.210 --> 00:14:20.610
to really take the
machine learning

00:14:20.610 --> 00:14:22.610
and put it into practical use.

00:14:22.610 --> 00:14:26.920
This is not a sci-fi project
anymore for enterprises.

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:30.300
And with all the experience
that we have gained

00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:34.050
and we continue to gain around
the world, what we are going

00:14:34.050 --> 00:14:37.470
to see in the next
decade is bigger

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:39.690
than the internet revolution.

00:14:39.690 --> 00:14:45.060
With that, I'm proud to bring on
stage Cisco, who will walk you

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:46.630
through their journey.

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:52.290
And please welcome Sanjiv Patel,
who leads the data sciences

00:14:52.290 --> 00:14:56.400
organization, as well as Vikram,
who leads the machine learning

00:14:56.400 --> 00:15:00.056
and AI practice at Cisco.

00:15:00.056 --> 00:15:01.012
Sanjiv, Vikram.

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:05.660
SANJIV PATEL: Thanks, Manju.

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:06.940
It's great to be here.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:08.810
As Manju said, I'm Sanjiv Patel.

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:10.760
I lead the data
science group that

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:13.370
focused on the customer,
partner, and sales experience.

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:14.619
VIKRAM VERMA: And I am Vikram.

00:15:14.619 --> 00:15:18.023
I am technical lead for
this particular program.

00:15:18.023 --> 00:15:19.398
SANJIV PATEL: So
today, I'm going

00:15:19.398 --> 00:15:22.722
to talk to you
about Omni-Channel

00:15:22.722 --> 00:15:24.430
and some of the
challenges that we've had

00:15:24.430 --> 00:15:26.910
and how we've worked
through those.

00:15:26.910 --> 00:15:30.730
And so I'll take you
back to a few years ago

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.420
when Vikram and I were
thinking about ways

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:36.730
to get our new customer
success organization started.

00:15:36.730 --> 00:15:40.410
And what we noted is that in
starting the customer success

00:15:40.410 --> 00:15:43.410
organization, we were
opening up many new channels.

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:45.000
And the challenge
that we were having

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:46.440
is that these
channels were often

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:49.780
disjointed from the other
channels that we had.

00:15:49.780 --> 00:15:53.190
And it potentially
lead to is customers

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:54.960
could engage with
certain channels

00:15:54.960 --> 00:15:57.120
and do that in a way
that was completely

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:00.300
separate from their
experience in another channel.

00:16:00.300 --> 00:16:02.070
And what we recognized
is that we really

00:16:02.070 --> 00:16:03.870
needed to tie the
experience together

00:16:03.870 --> 00:16:05.480
from these different channels.

00:16:05.480 --> 00:16:08.010
Now, I'm guessing Cisco
isn't alone there, right?

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:09.990
That's probably a
pretty common experience

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:13.320
in most B2B companies because
B2B companies are traditionally

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:15.990
not focused on this
aspect as much.

00:16:15.990 --> 00:16:20.250
And as the number of channels
has exploded over recent years

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:23.490
and the desire to connect
those channels has increased,

00:16:23.490 --> 00:16:25.500
as well as the
desire of customers

00:16:25.500 --> 00:16:30.000
to have more of a B2C experience
in all of their interactions

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.830
has increased, it's
put a lot of pressure

00:16:31.830 --> 00:16:33.070
on companies to change.

00:16:33.070 --> 00:16:34.530
And so we recognized
that we needed

00:16:34.530 --> 00:16:36.990
to do something that
would allow us to bring

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:39.240
the experience
together in some way,

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:41.470
and so that regardless
of the channel

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:44.440
the customer chose to
engage with us through,

00:16:44.440 --> 00:16:47.800
they would have a consistent
and personalized experience.

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:49.980
What we really
wanted to make sure

00:16:49.980 --> 00:16:52.980
took place is that
when a customer used

00:16:52.980 --> 00:16:55.350
a certain channel,
that channel was

00:16:55.350 --> 00:16:57.570
able to interact with the
customer in a way that

00:16:57.570 --> 00:16:59.400
considered all of the
other interactions

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:01.650
that that customer
had had with Cisco

00:17:01.650 --> 00:17:04.710
and brought the most
amount of information

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:06.390
back into that
engagement so that it

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:08.537
was an intelligent,
personalized experience.

00:17:11.160 --> 00:17:13.920
And that leads me to a couple
of quotes that are, I think,

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:15.000
very relevant.

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:18.150
One, talking about how important
that customer experience

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.880
has gotten to the
point where it is even

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:23.609
maybe more important
than product and price

00:17:23.609 --> 00:17:25.109
as a differentiator.

00:17:25.109 --> 00:17:27.960
The other one being
that AI is playing

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.851
a bigger and bigger role
in the customer experience.

00:17:30.851 --> 00:17:32.850
And that's certainly
something that Vikram and I

00:17:32.850 --> 00:17:35.625
felt was the case when
we started this journey.

00:17:35.625 --> 00:17:37.500
And we knew that we
needed to build something

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:39.910
based on an AI foundation.

00:17:39.910 --> 00:17:43.150
So that's what we want
to share with you today.

00:17:43.150 --> 00:17:46.110
First, let me describe
some of the challenges

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:49.770
that we had as we were looking
at the problem statement.

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:51.670
So first, as I've
mentioned already,

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:54.570
we had a desire to have an
Omni-Channel experience that

00:17:54.570 --> 00:17:58.670
was connected, but we saw that
there were certain silos--

00:17:58.670 --> 00:18:01.170
not everything was fragmented--
but there were certain silos

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:02.430
that we needed to figure out.

00:18:02.430 --> 00:18:06.100
How do we pull those back in as
part of an overall experience?

00:18:06.100 --> 00:18:09.300
And by the way, that includes
not only just digital channels,

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:11.220
but it also includes
sales channels,

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.410
people who are
engaging with customers

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:15.180
and making sure that
those people have

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:18.420
the same type of interaction
and that's consistent.

00:18:18.420 --> 00:18:21.510
The other one was siloed
campaign execution.

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:22.980
And what I mean
here is that as we

00:18:22.980 --> 00:18:24.720
would bring a
channel online, there

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:27.280
would be a team of people
working to enable it.

00:18:27.280 --> 00:18:30.210
But sometimes they didn't have
the right information from what

00:18:30.210 --> 00:18:32.880
was going on in other channels
and they were optimizing

00:18:32.880 --> 00:18:34.510
for that particular channel.

00:18:34.510 --> 00:18:37.110
So that led to a
siloed approach.

00:18:37.110 --> 00:18:39.990
And that also led to a lot
of labor-intensive efforts.

00:18:39.990 --> 00:18:42.810
As people would try
to ramp up a channel,

00:18:42.810 --> 00:18:45.420
they would try to gather
data independently

00:18:45.420 --> 00:18:48.120
from other channels and
bring that together.

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:50.730
So as channels grew
linearly, we almost

00:18:50.730 --> 00:18:52.230
found that the
number of people that

00:18:52.230 --> 00:18:54.810
were involved in
supporting those channels

00:18:54.810 --> 00:18:55.656
grew exponentially.

00:18:55.656 --> 00:18:57.030
And we wanted to
figure out a way

00:18:57.030 --> 00:18:59.850
that we could
address that issue.

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:04.080
A core part of the issue for us
was the scattered data issue.

00:19:04.080 --> 00:19:05.580
We weren't pulling
all of this data

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:07.380
from these different
channel interactions

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:08.796
as well as new
data that we wanted

00:19:08.796 --> 00:19:11.999
to include, such as customer
interactions and our products,

00:19:11.999 --> 00:19:14.040
together in a way that
could support the customer

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:15.510
experience.

00:19:15.510 --> 00:19:17.760
And as a result, we didn't
have the amount of insights

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:21.150
that we wanted to, and we
felt like we could do more

00:19:21.150 --> 00:19:24.060
around the intelligence,
around those interactions

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:25.800
to make them more
useful to customers

00:19:25.800 --> 00:19:29.720
and improve their experience.

00:19:29.720 --> 00:19:32.230
So what we recognized,
as I mentioned,

00:19:32.230 --> 00:19:35.330
is we can't do this if we're
going to build it effectively

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:37.585
through just human
decision making.

00:19:37.585 --> 00:19:40.640
It was too complex given the
portfolio that Cisco has,

00:19:40.640 --> 00:19:42.620
the size of the
company, the diversity

00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.140
of the types of customers,
and their expectations

00:19:45.140 --> 00:19:47.160
around the experience
that they would get.

00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.110
And so purely human
decision making

00:19:49.110 --> 00:19:50.697
was not going to
solve the problem.

00:19:50.697 --> 00:19:52.280
And what we felt
like we really needed

00:19:52.280 --> 00:19:56.150
was a strategy that
allowed us to have

00:19:56.150 --> 00:19:58.490
a path to something scalable.

00:19:58.490 --> 00:20:00.830
And so there were three
pillars of that strategy--

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:05.210
connected data, connected
intelligence, and then

00:20:05.210 --> 00:20:06.230
automation.

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:10.120
And so I'll give you examples
of each one of those in turn

00:20:10.120 --> 00:20:12.754
so you have a sense of
what we're talking about.

00:20:12.754 --> 00:20:15.170
So the first one is around
connecting data and getting it.

00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:16.610
And that's a pretty
obvious point

00:20:16.610 --> 00:20:19.140
that it's important to get data.

00:20:19.140 --> 00:20:22.250
However, part of what
I want to talk about

00:20:22.250 --> 00:20:25.070
is the way we were gathering
data and connecting it,

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:26.720
which I think is
the important part.

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:31.080
And it's where the machine
learning piece really relates.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:34.680
So we were inspired by some
examples in the industry

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:37.380
where they were using machine
learning to connect data.

00:20:37.380 --> 00:20:39.840
One of the ones that I
very specifically remember

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:41.170
is around hospitals.

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:44.070
So hospitals do a good job
connecting data together

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:45.660
using machine learning.

00:20:45.660 --> 00:20:48.780
It's not possible for them to
match purely on Social Security

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:49.380
numbers.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:52.230
Sometimes people make up
Social Security numbers.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:56.070
It's not possible to match
on just patient names.

00:20:56.070 --> 00:20:57.722
Sometimes those
names are identical.

00:20:57.722 --> 00:20:59.430
And so they use machine
learning as a way

00:20:59.430 --> 00:21:01.620
to make sure that the
highest likelihood

00:21:01.620 --> 00:21:03.530
match is taking place.

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:06.210
And they can do that
very fast and scalably.

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:09.960
Well, similarly, we decided
to use that approach

00:21:09.960 --> 00:21:12.570
in connecting some of our
customer data together.

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:16.830
And so whether it was connecting
contacts back to accounts,

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.410
whether it was actually
connecting different channel

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:21.750
data together from
different groups that

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:25.020
didn't traditionally
fit together very well

00:21:25.020 --> 00:21:27.810
or even doing kind of
operational matching of data,

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:29.790
we started to really
embrace this approach

00:21:29.790 --> 00:21:31.060
of connecting data.

00:21:31.060 --> 00:21:34.860
And what we found is that
it was three big advantages.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:37.380
One is it was much
more accurate.

00:21:37.380 --> 00:21:39.690
This chart gives
you a sense of some

00:21:39.690 --> 00:21:42.240
of the accuracy that we
found when we were comparing

00:21:42.240 --> 00:21:45.360
the human-based approach to
the machine-learning-based

00:21:45.360 --> 00:21:46.170
approach.

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:48.630
It was also much more fast.

00:21:48.630 --> 00:21:50.130
So we were able
to do this quickly

00:21:50.130 --> 00:21:51.930
within a matter of
days, as opposed

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:53.680
to taking a lot
longer than that.

00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:55.270
And it was very
flexible to change.

00:21:55.270 --> 00:21:59.610
So as data changed, which
changes pretty much constantly,

00:21:59.610 --> 00:22:02.040
you didn't have to go back
through a human process

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:03.990
to figure out how to
deal with that change.

00:22:03.990 --> 00:22:07.300
You were actually able to adapt
to that change very quickly.

00:22:07.300 --> 00:22:08.934
And so these are
all big advantages.

00:22:08.934 --> 00:22:10.350
And the connecting
the data became

00:22:10.350 --> 00:22:13.570
foundational to the intelligence
work that we wanted to do.

00:22:13.570 --> 00:22:16.200
The other thing that we
spent a lot of time doing

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:18.870
is around understanding intent.

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:19.706
And so I think--

00:22:19.706 --> 00:22:20.580
we're here at Google.

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:23.790
Google does that really well
with their search engine.

00:22:23.790 --> 00:22:28.639
If you go in and you type
in the letters C-A-L, CAL,

00:22:28.639 --> 00:22:30.180
into the Google
search engine, Google

00:22:30.180 --> 00:22:32.596
knows that you're looking for
the University of California

00:22:32.596 --> 00:22:36.040
at Berkeley, which is not
intuitive to people who are

00:22:36.040 --> 00:22:37.809
outside of the United States.

00:22:37.809 --> 00:22:40.350
And the reason they know that
is because millions of searches

00:22:40.350 --> 00:22:43.710
has been done in which
intent has been established.

00:22:43.710 --> 00:22:46.770
Well, similarly, we
started looking at problems

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.620
that we could apply a
similar type of logic to.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:51.130
And there were many of them.

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:54.330
One of them is the
fact that at Cisco, we

00:22:54.330 --> 00:22:56.400
have a broad
portfolio of products,

00:22:56.400 --> 00:22:59.370
and we tend to sell solutions
to business customers.

00:22:59.370 --> 00:23:01.750
And those solutions are not
just one specific product.

00:23:01.750 --> 00:23:04.890
They tend to be groups of
products all sold together.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:08.330
And one of our problems
was establishing,

00:23:08.330 --> 00:23:11.460
when has a solution been
sold to a customer that

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:13.360
is the same for two
different customers,

00:23:13.360 --> 00:23:17.040
meaning customer A may have
bought products A, B, C, and D.

00:23:17.040 --> 00:23:22.470
Customer B may have bought
products E, F, G, and H.

00:23:22.470 --> 00:23:24.520
But they may be
the same solution.

00:23:24.520 --> 00:23:27.550
And so how do we match those
two as the same solution?

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:31.230
So what we did is we looked at
five million deals in which we

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:33.060
looked at what
customers had purchased,

00:23:33.060 --> 00:23:36.484
and we started looking at the
groupings of those products,

00:23:36.484 --> 00:23:37.650
not just what was purchased.

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:42.230
But then what do multiple
people purchase when

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:44.130
they're purchasing from Cisco?

00:23:44.130 --> 00:23:46.080
And how do they group together?

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:48.730
And we were able to use that
in a number of different ways.

00:23:48.730 --> 00:23:51.570
First, the grouping became
an input into things

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:53.880
like our models.

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:57.526
It also became a good
thing to augment feedback

00:23:57.526 --> 00:23:59.400
from product managers
who were telling us how

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:01.480
to look at solutions at Cisco.

00:24:01.480 --> 00:24:04.170
And we were able to use it in a
number of other things as well.

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:06.510
But this became some
foundational work

00:24:06.510 --> 00:24:08.880
we could do from a data
side in preparation

00:24:08.880 --> 00:24:13.470
for the next step, which
was the intelligence piece.

00:24:13.470 --> 00:24:15.790
Next, I want to talk about
the scalable intelligence.

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:20.700
And what I'm going to go
into is the AI and machine

00:24:20.700 --> 00:24:22.526
learning aspect of things.

00:24:22.526 --> 00:24:24.150
The thing that I want
to emphasize here

00:24:24.150 --> 00:24:27.630
is that there's two
strategies that we had.

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:29.370
One was around inbound.

00:24:29.370 --> 00:24:32.280
So when customers
come to us, we want

00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:36.240
a way to classify what
their interest is.

00:24:36.240 --> 00:24:40.080
And we want a way to respond to
it as automatically as possible

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:41.634
and as intelligently
as possible,

00:24:41.634 --> 00:24:43.800
taking into account all the
information that we have

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.680
about them through things
like a chatbot or something

00:24:46.680 --> 00:24:48.747
like those types of mechanisms.

00:24:48.747 --> 00:24:50.580
We also, however, wanted
to be able to route

00:24:50.580 --> 00:24:54.070
those inbound
interactions to people.

00:24:54.070 --> 00:24:56.310
So if there are people
who should be involved,

00:24:56.310 --> 00:24:59.670
we want the ability to do the
routing to the right person.

00:24:59.670 --> 00:25:01.350
And then we want
the person to be

00:25:01.350 --> 00:25:03.450
enabled with the right
set of priorities

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:05.790
around how to respond
to those and what

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:09.190
to say in the interactions to
be as successful as possible.

00:25:09.190 --> 00:25:13.990
So that's how we're thinking
about our inbound AI vision,

00:25:13.990 --> 00:25:15.160
if you will.

00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:16.950
From an outbound
perspective, we want

00:25:16.950 --> 00:25:21.240
to be able to make sure that
when we're pushing out things

00:25:21.240 --> 00:25:23.250
like emails to customers,
we want to make sure

00:25:23.250 --> 00:25:26.290
that it's part of a journey.

00:25:26.290 --> 00:25:28.320
What we want to be able
to do is increasingly

00:25:28.320 --> 00:25:30.750
make that a data-science-driven
journey, meaning

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:36.960
that we are determining the
right content, the right timing

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:41.880
for that content, and the right
to positioning in that content

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:44.900
so that the customer ultimately
is getting what they want.

00:25:44.900 --> 00:25:47.310
And we are making
sure that we're

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:49.710
engaging with that
customer in a manner that

00:25:49.710 --> 00:25:52.440
makes sense for them,
as opposed to it's

00:25:52.440 --> 00:25:54.250
a generic journey for us.

00:25:54.250 --> 00:25:57.530
And so this development of
data-science-driven journeys

00:25:57.530 --> 00:26:00.930
is something that we're still
working on and in the process

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:01.456
of doing.

00:26:01.456 --> 00:26:03.330
So let me share a couple
of examples with you

00:26:03.330 --> 00:26:05.621
around sort of some of the
things we're doing around AI

00:26:05.621 --> 00:26:09.160
and machine learning that
I think may be interesting.

00:26:09.160 --> 00:26:10.550
The first one is around Amazon.

00:26:10.550 --> 00:26:12.930
And Amazon predicts
shopper interest

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:14.650
based on their behavior.

00:26:14.650 --> 00:26:16.650
And this is very well known.

00:26:16.650 --> 00:26:18.510
Everybody knows that
Amazon does this.

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:22.100
What I found interesting
is that Amazon, Airbnb,

00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:23.850
and many different
companies, Spotify,

00:26:23.850 --> 00:26:27.540
all describe how
much of their revenue

00:26:27.540 --> 00:26:30.750
is actually based on their
recommendation engine.

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:33.390
And that's, I think,
a fascinating thing

00:26:33.390 --> 00:26:35.670
in terms of how
important these are.

00:26:35.670 --> 00:26:37.940
So we built a recommendation
engine as well,

00:26:37.940 --> 00:26:40.450
looking at historical
product, purchasing behavior,

00:26:40.450 --> 00:26:42.720
and what customers had
in their installed base.

00:26:42.720 --> 00:26:45.600
And the thing that stands
out to me, in addition

00:26:45.600 --> 00:26:48.390
to the ROI of the
model, is just how many

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:51.930
different places it's been
deployed across Cisco.

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:58.650
And so from emails, to display
ads, to online, even to sales

00:26:58.650 --> 00:27:02.340
recommendations, we were using
it in a broad variety of ways

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:06.549
so that we can get as much
leverage out of it as possible

00:27:06.549 --> 00:27:08.340
and think about what
the importance of that

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:11.170
is in terms of a consistent
customer experience.

00:27:11.170 --> 00:27:13.440
Now you have
numerous channels all

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:15.870
having conversations
with the customers,

00:27:15.870 --> 00:27:18.810
and having similar upsell
conversations and that sort

00:27:18.810 --> 00:27:20.320
of thing with customers.

00:27:20.320 --> 00:27:23.160
It's a great way to
drive consistency.

00:27:23.160 --> 00:27:25.470
The other example is, and
I'll use Google again,

00:27:25.470 --> 00:27:29.880
Google uses Google Maps and can
provide optimal routes for you.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:32.070
This optimization
piece, well, that's

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:34.870
a big piece of what we want
to be able to do as well.

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:37.380
And so one of the
things that we have done

00:27:37.380 --> 00:27:40.335
is look at how we can
use machine learning

00:27:40.335 --> 00:27:44.400
and AI to predict what
the best channel to engage

00:27:44.400 --> 00:27:46.050
a customer is going to be.

00:27:46.050 --> 00:27:47.550
And so I mentioned
that a little bit

00:27:47.550 --> 00:27:50.430
earlier in the inbound and
outbound visions of how we

00:27:50.430 --> 00:27:53.360
were building our AI roadmap.

00:27:53.360 --> 00:27:56.130
And so, in this case, we were
scoring in customer engagement

00:27:56.130 --> 00:27:57.854
through a variety of
different channels

00:27:57.854 --> 00:27:59.520
and effectively
predicting how we should

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:00.820
be engaging with that customer.

00:28:00.820 --> 00:28:02.694
So for example, in this
case, if the customer

00:28:02.694 --> 00:28:05.550
has a very high email score,
great, we'll use email.

00:28:05.550 --> 00:28:08.470
But if it's a low email score
and something else is bigger,

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:10.527
well, we probably won't
email that customer

00:28:10.527 --> 00:28:12.360
because we know it's
going to be effectively

00:28:12.360 --> 00:28:15.730
not valuable for that customer
and seek to engage them

00:28:15.730 --> 00:28:17.955
through other means.

00:28:17.955 --> 00:28:21.220
The last thing I want to
talk about is automation.

00:28:21.220 --> 00:28:24.700
And automation is, I
think, probably the hardest

00:28:24.700 --> 00:28:27.530
piece of this, which is, how
do you take those insights

00:28:27.530 --> 00:28:30.210
and how do you put them into
these different channels

00:28:30.210 --> 00:28:31.510
so that they work?

00:28:31.510 --> 00:28:35.560
And so I'll describe at a high
level our approach to this.

00:28:35.560 --> 00:28:37.090
Effectively, what
we're doing is we

00:28:37.090 --> 00:28:40.390
have a group piloting
as new channels are

00:28:40.390 --> 00:28:44.800
onboarded-- piloting
the use of these models.

00:28:44.800 --> 00:28:48.070
And then we have a group
that's operationalizing that.

00:28:48.070 --> 00:28:51.490
So I'll talk about that
for just a brief moment.

00:28:51.490 --> 00:28:53.330
We've got, effectively,
two groups.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:56.350
And I found that this is a
really good practice for us.

00:28:56.350 --> 00:28:59.020
We've got one group which is
a global business team that

00:28:59.020 --> 00:29:01.180
seeks to work with
channel owners,

00:29:01.180 --> 00:29:03.970
understand what they're
trying to accomplish,

00:29:03.970 --> 00:29:06.610
has the understanding of
the models that we have

00:29:06.610 --> 00:29:08.470
so they can bring it
to the channel owner

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:11.170
and give them options in
terms of how they might want

00:29:11.170 --> 00:29:14.050
to engage with customers,
and then comes back to us

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:17.850
and provides sometimes
requests, sometimes ask

00:29:17.850 --> 00:29:20.450
requests for support,
that sort of thing.

00:29:20.450 --> 00:29:23.230
And so frequently, we use
things, even something

00:29:23.230 --> 00:29:24.730
as basic as an
Excel spreadsheet,

00:29:24.730 --> 00:29:26.320
just to get started
and understand

00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:29.000
how we can serve the people
in that channel better.

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:32.110
Then we have a
deployment team that

00:29:32.110 --> 00:29:34.210
is made up of data
engineers that's focused

00:29:34.210 --> 00:29:36.909
on automating that piece.

00:29:36.909 --> 00:29:38.950
So how do we get it in so
that these human beings

00:29:38.950 --> 00:29:43.180
don't have to be involved
in all of the process?

00:29:43.180 --> 00:29:45.940
That team that's working on the
automation is also working on,

00:29:45.940 --> 00:29:49.090
how do we drive a regular
cadence of A/B testing

00:29:49.090 --> 00:29:51.010
and experimentation
so we can make

00:29:51.010 --> 00:29:54.110
the engagement in that
channel as robust as possible?

00:29:54.110 --> 00:29:56.920
So with that, I will
hand it over to Vikram

00:29:56.920 --> 00:30:00.442
to talk about some of the
ways that we're handling this

00:30:00.442 --> 00:30:01.900
from an infrastructure
perspective.

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:03.233
VIKRAM VERMA: Thank you, Sanjiv.

00:30:03.233 --> 00:30:07.790
And I will start with
Jeff Bezos's comment.

00:30:07.790 --> 00:30:12.010
You said, AI is a horizontal
enabling layer and there is no

00:30:12.010 --> 00:30:15.010
aspect of the business where
AI cannot add to value.

00:30:15.010 --> 00:30:18.130
Now, going back to Sanjiv's
point about what we are doing

00:30:18.130 --> 00:30:20.740
and how we are doing--

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:23.200
when Sanjiv and I started
to look at the vision

00:30:23.200 --> 00:30:25.690
into technical aspect,
we started to lay down,

00:30:25.690 --> 00:30:27.640
what does it mean from
a technical requirement

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:28.570
point of view?

00:30:28.570 --> 00:30:30.280
We had hundreds of
thing, but I will just

00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:32.060
highlight four of them.

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:33.640
First one was get more data.

00:30:33.640 --> 00:30:36.970
Second one is build as
many models as we can.

00:30:36.970 --> 00:30:40.650
Third one is double
down on our data points.

00:30:40.650 --> 00:30:42.800
Some of the data points
that in B2B model

00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:45.420
we have not looked at it,
like user behavior, user

00:30:45.420 --> 00:30:46.476
interactions.

00:30:46.476 --> 00:30:48.100
Some places we are
good in the company.

00:30:48.100 --> 00:30:49.090
Some places we are not.

00:30:49.090 --> 00:30:50.920
How do we make
that as a practice,

00:30:50.920 --> 00:30:55.060
get that data to enable
the better working models

00:30:55.060 --> 00:30:57.100
for our stakeholders?

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:01.570
And the fourth thing is not
only building the models,

00:31:01.570 --> 00:31:03.220
giving it to the
users, but how do

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:06.070
we drive that personalization
that Sanjiv talked about,

00:31:06.070 --> 00:31:07.870
a real dynamic
personalization where

00:31:07.870 --> 00:31:10.420
we can integrate these
data science models

00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:13.630
or these AI techniques like
[INAUDIBLE] and chatbot

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:17.110
like Sanjiv talked about
in our real-user experience

00:31:17.110 --> 00:31:19.450
across the different channels?

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:21.250
We know that historically
we always used

00:31:21.250 --> 00:31:22.900
to know where the customer was.

00:31:22.900 --> 00:31:26.050
Now what we want to know is
where the customer will be

00:31:26.050 --> 00:31:28.347
and what do we need to
suggest to that customer?

00:31:28.347 --> 00:31:30.430
And if that customer is
taking a different action,

00:31:30.430 --> 00:31:32.580
then dynamically change
that recommendation.

00:31:32.580 --> 00:31:35.080
And by recommendation, it's not
only product recommendation,

00:31:35.080 --> 00:31:37.060
its about what we
show them, what

00:31:37.060 --> 00:31:38.710
we want to bring
to their attention,

00:31:38.710 --> 00:31:40.410
and what actions
do we want to drive

00:31:40.410 --> 00:31:41.890
based on those interactions.

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:44.530
So based on this
requirement, when

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:46.600
we looked at what do we
really need technically

00:31:46.600 --> 00:31:50.740
to do those things, we
needed an environment

00:31:50.740 --> 00:31:53.790
over our excellent
environment within Cisco,

00:31:53.790 --> 00:31:58.270
which can scale for a lot of
data where the data [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:58.270 --> 00:32:00.320
and integration is easier.

00:32:00.320 --> 00:32:02.540
It is not too complicated.

00:32:02.540 --> 00:32:05.320
We have a dynamic where we
can generate this data science

00:32:05.320 --> 00:32:07.330
model and deploy
them effectively.

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:10.100
And third thing is, how
can we put a microservices

00:32:10.100 --> 00:32:13.590
around this data science
environment which

00:32:13.590 --> 00:32:17.470
can provide the concurrent
A/B test pre-built in it so

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:20.480
that this whole environment
could come together and help

00:32:20.480 --> 00:32:22.680
our experience end to end?

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:24.070
This was a complicated ask.

00:32:24.070 --> 00:32:27.130
We looked at multiple
windows, and then we

00:32:27.130 --> 00:32:32.020
decided to go with
Google Cloud [INAUDIBLE]..

00:32:32.020 --> 00:32:34.360
And we will continue to use
our internal environment

00:32:34.360 --> 00:32:35.230
that we have.

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:37.860
But we are going to supplement
with this all trial experiment

00:32:37.860 --> 00:32:40.600
that Sanjiv mentioned on
the Google environment

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:42.640
and bring those learning
back in our experience

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:45.220
how we interact
with our customers.

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:47.920
And when I say customer, it's
the real customers, our sellers

00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:50.950
and other partners, all
the three dimensions.

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:55.930
If you look at our architecture
that we are working towards,

00:32:55.930 --> 00:32:58.480
majority of these pieces
are already in place.

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:00.220
But like any other
company, we have

00:33:00.220 --> 00:33:02.830
slow-moving, slow-changing data,
which we call the reference

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:05.820
information, and
fast-paced data which

00:33:05.820 --> 00:33:07.630
is transactional structure.

00:33:07.630 --> 00:33:10.510
And we had a lot of data
sources from our third party,

00:33:10.510 --> 00:33:11.440
from our application.

00:33:11.440 --> 00:33:13.400
We had even [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:13.400 --> 00:33:15.180
So when we looked
at it, we've tried

00:33:15.180 --> 00:33:16.967
to come up with
different strategy of how

00:33:16.967 --> 00:33:19.660
to ingest these data
points within Google Cloud.

00:33:19.660 --> 00:33:20.920
And we are using pub/sub.

00:33:20.920 --> 00:33:24.160
We are using GCP
as a [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:27.890
We are using the realtime
data transfer as well

00:33:27.890 --> 00:33:29.770
and putting that
into Google so we

00:33:29.770 --> 00:33:33.100
can build those scalable
models at a scale

00:33:33.100 --> 00:33:36.450
and enable them through
micro services environment.

00:33:36.450 --> 00:33:40.150
So as Sanjiv talked about,
we have implemented energy.

00:33:40.150 --> 00:33:42.700
We are taking it
to Google Cloud.

00:33:42.700 --> 00:33:45.070
We have all these data
sources, which we have already

00:33:45.070 --> 00:33:46.240
moved to Google Cloud.

00:33:46.240 --> 00:33:50.650
And we are working on Apache
[? B, ?] pretty much pub/sub,

00:33:50.650 --> 00:33:53.740
to take these third-party events
and bring it to Google Cloud

00:33:53.740 --> 00:33:58.090
so it can improve on our
data science capabilities

00:33:58.090 --> 00:34:01.310
and enable that experience
that we just talked about.

00:34:01.310 --> 00:34:05.350
If you are next generation
firewall [? ice ?] and customer

00:34:05.350 --> 00:34:08.079
of Cisco, you can see
that difference even now.

00:34:08.079 --> 00:34:09.620
We have already
taken the first step.

00:34:09.620 --> 00:34:12.310
When you log into Cisco.com,
on those product pages,

00:34:12.310 --> 00:34:14.040
you will see those
customizations.

00:34:14.040 --> 00:34:17.260
And a lot of things
are coming in future

00:34:17.260 --> 00:34:20.949
pretty soon as we are
moving on this journey.

00:34:20.949 --> 00:34:25.960
With this, I would say there
are a lot of things that we did.

00:34:25.960 --> 00:34:29.139
And some of the things in
the Google Cloud as a POC

00:34:29.139 --> 00:34:31.360
which we did was
sentiment analysis

00:34:31.360 --> 00:34:33.500
using Google's pre-built API.

00:34:33.500 --> 00:34:35.139
Google provides an
environment where

00:34:35.139 --> 00:34:36.580
we can analyze the phone call.

00:34:36.580 --> 00:34:38.739
We can analyze user behavior.

00:34:38.739 --> 00:34:42.130
We can analyze a sentiment
based on what we are getting

00:34:42.130 --> 00:34:43.659
or the feedback from the user.

00:34:43.659 --> 00:34:47.920
That provides us faster go
to market and a capability

00:34:47.920 --> 00:34:50.919
where we can
personalize and meet

00:34:50.919 --> 00:34:52.989
our objectives much faster.

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:55.600
With this, that
wraps our session.

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:58.910
Stay tuned for Q&amp;A, and we
will be back in a minute.

00:34:58.910 --> 00:34:59.410
Thank you.

00:35:55.981 --> 00:35:56.730
MANJU DEVADAS: OK.

00:35:56.730 --> 00:35:59.540
Thanks for listening
through the session so far.

00:35:59.540 --> 00:36:01.700
You know, we see some
questions coming up,

00:36:01.700 --> 00:36:03.485
and we'll take a few of them.

00:36:03.485 --> 00:36:07.530
Between myself and Sanjiv, we'll
tag team along to answer these.

00:36:07.530 --> 00:36:10.200
The first question is, do you
need a large amount of data

00:36:10.200 --> 00:36:13.770
to run AI predictions and
recommendations model?

00:36:13.770 --> 00:36:17.440
How can smaller businesses
and less customer data

00:36:17.440 --> 00:36:19.570
benefit from such models?

00:36:19.570 --> 00:36:22.030
As a general rule
of thumb, yes, the

00:36:22.030 --> 00:36:24.790
larger the amount
of data, the better.

00:36:24.790 --> 00:36:26.450
But there's no
definitive measure

00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:28.900
on how much exactly you need.

00:36:28.900 --> 00:36:31.370
It's a blend of
knowing your business.

00:36:31.370 --> 00:36:33.820
It's a blend of what you are
trying to build in your model

00:36:33.820 --> 00:36:36.100
and what kind of insights
you are trying to get.

00:36:36.100 --> 00:36:38.800
And is there enough
pattern in the data?

00:36:38.800 --> 00:36:42.580
So these are some three
or four key aspects

00:36:42.580 --> 00:36:45.160
that you have to look at
before judging whether you

00:36:45.160 --> 00:36:47.110
have enough data or not.

00:36:47.110 --> 00:36:50.440
In my experience working with
various customers' workloads,

00:36:50.440 --> 00:36:51.550
extremely large--

00:36:51.550 --> 00:36:53.740
literally, like a fortune 10--

00:36:53.740 --> 00:36:57.560
to the extremely small,
which is like SNB.

00:36:57.560 --> 00:37:02.410
And interestingly, depending on
the business urgency and need,

00:37:02.410 --> 00:37:05.980
both the spectrum of businesses
use machine learning.

00:37:05.980 --> 00:37:08.340
And for example online
e-tailers, as they call,

00:37:08.340 --> 00:37:11.890
online retailers, extremely
small business but extremely

00:37:11.890 --> 00:37:14.635
fast in adopting machine
learning with streaming data

00:37:14.635 --> 00:37:16.630
of customer reviews and so on.

00:37:16.630 --> 00:37:18.610
Other spectrum, the
large enterprises,

00:37:18.610 --> 00:37:21.850
also look for this because they
have a massive amount of data.

00:37:21.850 --> 00:37:24.950
That's all I'll answer
for that question.

00:37:24.950 --> 00:37:26.867
So Sanjiv, you want to
take the next question?

00:37:26.867 --> 00:37:27.658
SANJIV PATEL: Yeah.

00:37:27.658 --> 00:37:29.560
So the next question
is, what data elements

00:37:29.560 --> 00:37:33.130
is Cisco looking for to
increase customer experience?

00:37:33.130 --> 00:37:36.880
So I'd say two big things come
to mind, generally speaking.

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:40.727
So one is data that comes
from customer interactions.

00:37:40.727 --> 00:37:42.310
I mean, that should
be pretty obvious.

00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:45.070
But let's take WeChat,
which is getting

00:37:45.070 --> 00:37:46.750
bigger and bigger in China.

00:37:46.750 --> 00:37:49.030
So as we're engaging
with customers on WeChat,

00:37:49.030 --> 00:37:50.920
we really should have
a way to make sure

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:53.620
that that data is
coming back into Cisco

00:37:53.620 --> 00:37:57.130
and we're making sure that we're
doing something with that data,

00:37:57.130 --> 00:38:00.400
with the thought that that data
should improve the experience

00:38:00.400 --> 00:38:01.840
and engagement in
other channels.

00:38:01.840 --> 00:38:03.460
So that's one side of things.

00:38:03.460 --> 00:38:05.230
The other side of
things is actually

00:38:05.230 --> 00:38:06.490
data from our products.

00:38:06.490 --> 00:38:09.850
So we collect a huge amount of
data back from our products.

00:38:09.850 --> 00:38:14.320
And increasingly, as we're
adding more and more products

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:16.450
to our portfolio, especially
in the software space,

00:38:16.450 --> 00:38:17.620
that's growing.

00:38:17.620 --> 00:38:19.930
So a big part of what
we're trying to do

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:22.660
is make sure that as
that data is coming back,

00:38:22.660 --> 00:38:24.820
we can use that to
improve the experience

00:38:24.820 --> 00:38:26.226
and to personalize it.

00:38:26.226 --> 00:38:27.850
If we know, for
example, that customers

00:38:27.850 --> 00:38:29.558
are trying to implement
certain features,

00:38:29.558 --> 00:38:32.920
for example, then we should
be able to use that in a way

00:38:32.920 --> 00:38:36.610
to help them so they can get
value from their products that

00:38:36.610 --> 00:38:38.470
much faster.

00:38:38.470 --> 00:38:39.290
MANJU DEVADAS: OK.

00:38:39.290 --> 00:38:40.340
Thanks, Sanjiv.

00:38:40.340 --> 00:38:42.860
So see the next question.

00:38:42.860 --> 00:38:45.630
This has two, three parts to it.

00:38:45.630 --> 00:38:49.100
How do we make
sure companies are

00:38:49.100 --> 00:38:54.190
getting maximum revenue using
AI from a marketing perspective?

00:38:54.190 --> 00:38:56.230
How to validate the results?

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:58.330
And how to make sure
you have the right data

00:38:58.330 --> 00:38:59.800
while training the model?

00:38:59.800 --> 00:39:02.630
This is a pretty
loaded question.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:05.440
Between myself and
Sanjiv, we'll answer this.

00:39:05.440 --> 00:39:07.290
First of all, ROI--

00:39:07.290 --> 00:39:10.420
remember I mentioned early on
that machine learning and AI

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:13.030
projects are a journey
and not a project?

00:39:13.030 --> 00:39:14.350
So keep that in mind.

00:39:14.350 --> 00:39:19.330
If I told you 15 years back,
or rather, in 1995, internet

00:39:19.330 --> 00:39:22.060
is going to change every
aspect of your enterprise

00:39:22.060 --> 00:39:24.400
and the work, for
most enterprise

00:39:24.400 --> 00:39:26.890
it would be pretty hard to
imagine how that would be.

00:39:26.890 --> 00:39:28.780
And today, nobody debates that.

00:39:28.780 --> 00:39:31.060
You have to keep that
kind of perspective

00:39:31.060 --> 00:39:33.200
when you invest in
machine learning and AI.

00:39:33.200 --> 00:39:35.770
Don't expect quick
returns or magic here.

00:39:35.770 --> 00:39:37.360
There is no magic.

00:39:37.360 --> 00:39:40.030
And it's a collective
learning of Google

00:39:40.030 --> 00:39:43.000
and many similar companies
that is now available for you

00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:44.310
to learn.

00:39:44.310 --> 00:39:47.410
Now, when you build
machine learning models,

00:39:47.410 --> 00:39:51.010
to validate the result, keep
one rule of thumb in mind.

00:39:51.010 --> 00:39:53.690
You're mimicking
human decision making.

00:39:53.690 --> 00:39:55.780
In other words,
humans already make

00:39:55.780 --> 00:39:57.670
a lot of decisions
sitting at the desk.

00:39:57.670 --> 00:39:59.960
If you take a business
problem or forecasting,

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:03.370
for example, how do you know
that the machine learning

00:40:03.370 --> 00:40:05.160
is predicting better than you?

00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:06.200
You train the models.

00:40:06.200 --> 00:40:07.300
You evaluate the model.

00:40:07.300 --> 00:40:09.420
During the evaluation
phase, check whether it

00:40:09.420 --> 00:40:10.780
is doing better than humans.

00:40:10.780 --> 00:40:12.850
Then you have your answer.

00:40:12.850 --> 00:40:15.460
And there's-- as you go into
building machine learning

00:40:15.460 --> 00:40:17.860
model, you'll understand
this even better.

00:40:17.860 --> 00:40:20.110
And the last thing
is, how do you

00:40:20.110 --> 00:40:22.150
make sure you have right data?

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:23.980
There is this
concept of clean data

00:40:23.980 --> 00:40:26.150
that you hear very
often in enterprise.

00:40:26.150 --> 00:40:29.140
And fundamentally, I don't
believe in clean data.

00:40:29.140 --> 00:40:30.640
Data exist the way they are.

00:40:30.640 --> 00:40:32.380
Imagine if Google
were to sit and clean

00:40:32.380 --> 00:40:34.540
every single data
that gets entered.

00:40:34.540 --> 00:40:37.000
They'll never be able
to get you the results.

00:40:37.000 --> 00:40:39.830
With that perspective-- I'm not
saying don't clean the data.

00:40:39.830 --> 00:40:42.220
Data appropriation
takes 60% to 70%

00:40:42.220 --> 00:40:45.140
of time building
machine learning models.

00:40:45.140 --> 00:40:49.160
What's much more important is
don't try to make it perfect.

00:40:49.160 --> 00:40:51.000
Keep moving it along.

00:40:51.000 --> 00:40:51.920
Let the model run.

00:40:51.920 --> 00:40:54.350
Let the model also
tell you a few things

00:40:54.350 --> 00:40:56.590
that you can improvise on.

00:40:56.590 --> 00:40:58.340
Sanjiv, do you want
to add anything to it?

00:40:58.340 --> 00:40:59.930
SANJIV PATEL: I totally
agree with your perspective,

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:00.596
actually, Manju.

00:41:00.596 --> 00:41:01.335
That's correct.

00:41:01.335 --> 00:41:02.210
MANJU DEVADAS: Great.

00:41:02.210 --> 00:41:03.140
You want to take the next one?

00:41:03.140 --> 00:41:03.810
SANJIV PATEL: Sure.

00:41:03.810 --> 00:41:05.570
So the next question
is, will the models

00:41:05.570 --> 00:41:09.980
that you're developing tell
the humans or virtual sales

00:41:09.980 --> 00:41:11.900
in real time of who to target?

00:41:11.900 --> 00:41:14.520
And how does it improve
conversion rate?

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:18.470
So another question that has
many different aspects to it.

00:41:18.470 --> 00:41:21.810
I think what I'm honing in on
in is this word "real time."

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:25.360
So yes, the models will tell
human beings whom to target.

00:41:25.360 --> 00:41:27.140
We are doing that already today.

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:29.760
The real time piece is the one
that we're working towards.

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:32.416
So for example, let's say the
best case scenario, Manju,

00:41:32.416 --> 00:41:34.040
you know, you're a
customer of Cisco's.

00:41:34.040 --> 00:41:36.110
And you come to Facebook.

00:41:36.110 --> 00:41:38.660
And you go, hey, I want
to buy a Cisco router.

00:41:38.660 --> 00:41:41.140
I would love to put in
someone's hands right away.

00:41:41.140 --> 00:41:42.380
We're not at that point yet.

00:41:42.380 --> 00:41:43.940
So we're working
towards, how do we

00:41:43.940 --> 00:41:46.404
get things into the
hands of salespeople

00:41:46.404 --> 00:41:48.320
as quickly as possible
when we know that there

00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:50.450
are opportunities to do so?

00:41:50.450 --> 00:41:53.960
In terms of how does it
improve conversion rate,

00:41:53.960 --> 00:41:57.560
we don't have the
specific metrics

00:41:57.560 --> 00:42:02.420
around how is conversion rate
improved from before or after.

00:42:02.420 --> 00:42:04.910
But one of the things
that we're looking at is--

00:42:04.910 --> 00:42:06.890
just general at this point--

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:08.450
anecdotal feedback from sales.

00:42:08.450 --> 00:42:12.050
Are they finding value in
what we're providing them?

00:42:12.050 --> 00:42:14.840
Is it generally leading to
sort of better outcomes or not?

00:42:14.840 --> 00:42:17.010
And so far, the feedback
has been very positive.

00:42:17.010 --> 00:42:19.250
So that's been great.

00:42:19.250 --> 00:42:21.320
And I'll take the
last one, as well.

00:42:21.320 --> 00:42:21.920
MANJU DEVADAS: Sure, go ahead.

00:42:21.920 --> 00:42:23.390
SANJIV PATEL: So
the last question

00:42:23.390 --> 00:42:26.420
is, how important is accuracy
in the models that you're

00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:27.350
developing?

00:42:27.350 --> 00:42:29.450
And what are the ways
to increase the accuracy

00:42:29.450 --> 00:42:30.460
of the propensity model?

00:42:30.460 --> 00:42:32.515
So that's a great question.

00:42:32.515 --> 00:42:34.640
And in fact, maybe the
answer might be a little bit

00:42:34.640 --> 00:42:38.060
counter-intuitive
in that we are not

00:42:38.060 --> 00:42:41.270
overly concerned about accuracy
in the models, at least

00:42:41.270 --> 00:42:42.290
initially.

00:42:42.290 --> 00:42:45.380
Our goal is to build models
and put them out there

00:42:45.380 --> 00:42:46.870
and to learn from them.

00:42:46.870 --> 00:42:50.690
And so sometimes a lot of
focus is placed-- especially

00:42:50.690 --> 00:42:53.690
when you're involved in
something like Kaggle,

00:42:53.690 --> 00:42:56.840
data science competitions--
how do you improve the accuracy

00:42:56.840 --> 00:43:00.020
from 74.1 to 74.2?

00:43:00.020 --> 00:43:01.520
And that's a huge improvement.

00:43:01.520 --> 00:43:03.530
We don't operate in
that kind of mindset.

00:43:03.530 --> 00:43:05.125
Our mindset is, let's
build the model.

00:43:05.125 --> 00:43:06.083
Let's put it out there.

00:43:06.083 --> 00:43:07.070
Let's see how it does.

00:43:07.070 --> 00:43:10.640
Is it doing is something that we
think will advance the customer

00:43:10.640 --> 00:43:11.990
experience in some way?

00:43:11.990 --> 00:43:14.660
And then over time,
iteratively, let's continue

00:43:14.660 --> 00:43:17.430
to improve it by
getting more data,

00:43:17.430 --> 00:43:21.080
by refining the model itself,
and finding out what works.

00:43:21.080 --> 00:43:22.970
That is the fastest
way for us to be

00:43:22.970 --> 00:43:27.530
able to make kind of the roadmap
that we talked about a reality.

00:43:27.530 --> 00:43:29.565
So that's how I would
answer that question.

00:43:29.565 --> 00:43:30.440
MANJU DEVADAS: Great.

00:43:30.440 --> 00:43:31.310
Thanks, Sanjiv.

00:43:31.310 --> 00:43:36.090
And the last comment from us
is, when you start the journey,

00:43:36.090 --> 00:43:38.040
start it in a small way.

00:43:38.040 --> 00:43:39.230
Learn, experiment.

00:43:39.230 --> 00:43:41.690
Google will help you,
along with Pluto7.

00:43:41.690 --> 00:43:45.066
You can conduct a workshop,
build an initial model,

00:43:45.066 --> 00:43:46.440
and get started
with the journey.

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:47.814
And from there
on, there is a lot

00:43:47.814 --> 00:43:50.340
of learning that comes
into your enterprise.

00:43:50.340 --> 00:43:52.360
And have fun.

00:43:52.360 --> 00:43:54.250
Thanks, Sanjiv and Vikram.

00:43:54.250 --> 00:43:58.790
And with that, stay tuned
for the next session, which

00:43:58.790 --> 00:44:02.160
is on from hype to value,
how to start machine learning

00:44:02.160 --> 00:44:02.760
projects.

00:44:02.760 --> 00:44:04.880
A fun topic, as well.

00:44:04.880 --> 00:44:07.520
Thank you.

00:44:07.520 --> 00:44:10.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

